  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Find the interval where the function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}} \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)\\,j+(1-{t}^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t):$  \n\n- We observe that $u(t)$  is differentiable on $(-1,1),$ as its derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1$ and the function is not defined for $|t|>1.$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\pi t)$,  is not defined for integer values of $t$ and hence it is not differentiable at $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026.$\n\n- The second and third components,   $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)$ and $1-{t}^{2},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-1,0)\\cup (0,1).$",
